WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:04.000
A year ago, I called myself a "vibe coder, and technically

2
00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:08.190
I still vibe code, which, wait, terrible name for what this

3
00:00:08.330 --> 00:00:10.750
actually is. But that's where I started.

4
00:00:11.210 --> 00:00:14.310
I don't sit down and manually write every line of software

5
00:00:14.390 --> 00:00:18.210
I build. I work with AI, I give agents problems, I

6
00:00:18.250 --> 00:00:21.880
direct them, I inspect what they produce, I reject a lot

7
00:00:21.890 --> 00:00:25.040
of it, I change the architecture, and we build again.

8
00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:28.020
But somewhere along the way, the problems changed.

9
00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:31.500
I stopped asking "How do I- I built this app and

10
00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:34.940
started asking: How should all of these systems work together?

11
00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:36.480
Who owns the memory?

12
00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:38.450
Which agent gets access?

13
00:00:38.890 --> 00:00:40.870
How does one agent hand work to another?

14
00:00:41.350 --> 00:00:43.490
How do I know an action actually happened?

15
00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:46.330
What stops an agent from exceeding its authority?

16
00:00:46.850 --> 00:00:48.950
And what does a human need to see to remain in

17
00:00:49.010 --> 00:00:53.720
control? That last one, that's the Jedi Council problem: Who gets

18
00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:55.340
a lightsaber? Who doesn't?

19
00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:56.640
Who thinks they should?

20
00:00:57.140 --> 00:01:00.000
Anyway, those weren't vibe coding problems anymore.

21
00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:04.280
They were systems problems, and solving them became Agentropolis.

22
00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:07.420
My role changed too; I don't need to be the best

23
00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:12.970
Python programmer, Rust engineer, product designer, and 3D developer simultaneously.

24
00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:14.470
That's not how I work.

25
00:01:14.990 --> 00:01:19.030
I build teams of intelligence around the problem: one agent researches,

26
00:01:19.410 --> 00:01:23.700
another implements, another reviews, another challenges the assumptions.

27
00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:26.960
Hermes provides persistent agents and coordination.

28
00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:30.000
Coding agents work directly against repositories.

29
00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:34.380
And I stay above the system, deciding what belongs, what doesn't,

30
00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:36.620
and where the architecture needs to change.

31
00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:41.410
AI doesn't eliminate expertise, it changes where the human expertise has

32
00:01:41.470 --> 00:01:44.610
to be applied. This is another part of working with AI

33
00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:45.950
that doesn't get shown enough.

34
00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:50.290
An agent can satisfy the requirements and still completely miss the

35
00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:55.220
idea. This version technically worked, the information was there, the interface

36
00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:58.200
rendered, the deployment worked, and I hated it.

37
00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:00.000
It looked like an enterprise app.

38
00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:03.440
This was the part in the movie where everybody else thinks

39
00:02:03.460 --> 00:02:06.520
the machine is working fine, and I'm yelling at the screen

40
00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:08.470
because I can already see act three.

41
00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:12.990
Agentropolis wasn't supposed to feel like an enterprise dashboard, so the

42
00:02:13.030 --> 00:02:15.710
problem wasn't code, the problem was direction.

43
00:02:16.310 --> 00:02:19.790
I pulled Parallax back up as the visual reference, rewrote the

44
00:02:19.830 --> 00:02:24.140
design canon, changed the acceptance criteria, and sent the system through

45
00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:27.920
another iteration. That's what directing AI actually looks like.

46
00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:30.000
Generation is cheap, judgement is cheap.

47
00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:35.130
Isn't. Parallax came from the same philosophy: never confuse generation with

48
00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:38.650
proof. An agent can tell me something is finished, I want

49
00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:42.640
the system to inspect the result, verify the objective, and leave

50
00:02:42.740 --> 00:02:46.040
evidence. C. Act. See again, prove it.

51
00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:49.970
That idea started with spatial environments, but it became one of

52
00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:54.610
my principles for agentic systems: generated doesn't equal verified.

53
00:02:55.030 --> 00:02:57.790
You don't hand somebody the Death Star and skip asking who

54
00:02:57.870 --> 00:03:01.280
has the keys. I also didn't build this inside a research

55
00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:04.960
laboratory. There wasn't an engineering department waiting downstairs.

56
00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:06.440
I'm a solo founder.

57
00:03:06.940 --> 00:03:11.440
That constraint forced a different question: how much organizational capability can

58
00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:13.380
one person assemble using agents?

59
00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:19.170
Research. Architecture, code, design, documentation, testing, operations,

60
00:03:19.270 --> 00:03:24.650
creative production, not by pretending I personally possess every specialization, but

61
00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:27.470
by learning how to coordinate machines that increasingly do.

62
00:03:28.190 --> 00:03:31.510
At that point it was basically "push it, except I was

63
00:03:31.590 --> 00:03:34.120
pushing agents across the entire architecture.

64
00:03:34.660 --> 00:03:36.580
Agentropolis became the experiment.

65
00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:39.440
So what do I actually call what I do now?

66
00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:44.340
Probably agentic systems architecture with a lot of creative technology mixed

67
00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:47.810
in. I work between the idea and the implementation.

68
00:03:48.370 --> 00:03:52.650
I turn emerging capabilities into systems, I design agent roles and

69
00:03:52.670 --> 00:03:56.950
authority boundaries, I decide what gets delegated to machines and what

70
00:03:57.030 --> 00:04:00.840
stays under human control, then I use agents to help execute

71
00:04:00.850 --> 00:04:05.340
the architecture. I'm still learning, the technology is still changing, but

72
00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:07.180
that's also why I'm building this now.

73
00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:10.600
I'm not convinced the next generation of software will look like

74
00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:14.480
today's applications. I think we're moving towards systems populated by By

75
00:04:14.540 --> 00:04:19.770
persistent agents, different models, different machines, different owners, different

76
00:04:19.810 --> 00:04:23.010
levels of authority, working together across environments.

77
00:04:23.510 --> 00:04:27.110
So now I've got agents talking to agents across machines, and

78
00:04:27.190 --> 00:04:30.560
I'm sitting there thinking, "Okay, this is starting to look less

79
00:04:30.620 --> 00:04:32.700
like an app and more like the rebel fleet.

80
00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:36.460
If that's where we're going, the hard problem isn't simply making

81
00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:40.360
the agents smarter, it's designing the systems they operate inside.

82
00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:42.380
That's the problem I'm interested in.

83
00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:44.380
That's what I've spent this year learning how to solve.

84
00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:48.230
How to build an agentropolis is my working answer, not a

85
00:04:48.310 --> 00:04:51.850
finished answer, a system that's alive enough to keep changing as

86
00:04:51.890 --> 00:04:55.730
the technology changes. I started this as a vibe coder, I

87
00:04:55.830 --> 00:04:59.230
ended up becoming an architect of agent systems, and I'm just

88
00:04:59.270 --> 00:04:59.910
getting started.
